WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:19.486
we will be talking
about a new topic today.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:22.389
And that's polymers.

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:25.725
And we're going to
start by talking about--

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.294
what I want to do
is cover two ways

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:31.731
that we humans make polymers.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:35.802
And the first way has to do with
something called the radical.

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:39.039
So that's where I want
to start our story.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.375
And what is a radical?

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:51.251
Well, a radical is
a molecule that has

00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:54.621
one or more unpaired electrons.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:01:05.432
So one or more
unpaired electrons.

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:08.568
But as you can imagine, if
I've got an unpaired electron,

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:11.905
it's kind of like a broken bond.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:13.239
It's kind of like a broken bond.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.207
That's a radical.

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:17.343
And you could also
imagine that a broken bond

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:20.513
wants to not be broken.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:24.717
So it's going to try to
find something to bond to.

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:28.388
That's why we've got radicals
all in us all the time.

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:30.623
Sometimes they're important,
but sometimes they

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:34.227
can cause a lot of harm.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:36.628
And that's why if you
eat blueberries, then

00:01:36.628 --> 00:01:40.633
you get these things
called antioxidants.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.336
And antioxidants, that's
just one of many, many types

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:44.404
of antioxidants.

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:45.238
What do they do?

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.973
They go and they
give these radicals

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:51.911
what they need, what they're
looking for, an electron.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.279
They give them an electron.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.048
And they don't mind.

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:57.317
They don't become
radical themselves.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:02:01.488
They just simply stop the
radical from being radical.

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:02.555
Now, why does this matter?

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:05.658
Well, we're going to see
what that means for polymers.

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:06.759
But let's make a radical.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.228
So imagine-- and I'm
going to go Lewis on us,

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.297
because I want to
see the electrons.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:12.232
I'm going to go Lewis.

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:14.901
So imagine I've got methane.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:20.039
Well, methane looks like
this, nice and happy.

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:21.040
Oh, the octet.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.414
But now I introduced
a chlorine atom.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.316
So I've got now chlorine.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:34.320
And chlorine is hungry.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.924
Chlorine wants to fill
its octet by itself.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.494
And so what does it do?

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.730
It's like, well, I'm powerful.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:44.230
I'm strong.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.432
I'm just going to
grab from methane.

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:48.735
It sees methane,
and it's like, OK,

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:51.804
you give me one
of your electrons.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:52.972
How about that is a deal?

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:57.877
And here we go.

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.946
And here we go.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:03.283
And it takes it in the
form of the hydrogen.

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:09.022
And so we got this like
this, and like this.

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:12.892
Oh, the chlorine
got really happy,

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:16.296
but look at what
happened to the methane.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:20.233
It lost an electron
and a hydrogen with it,

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:23.870
and it has an unpaired electron.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.371
That electron is not happy.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:29.642
I mean, it just wants
to bond to something.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:30.143
That's all.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.245
You can't blame it.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.914
Don't we all want that?

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:40.053
The problem is that this
really needs it badly.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:41.754
And so this is called--

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.190
so what we did is
we took chlorine,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.293
and we combined it with methane.

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:50.797
And we made what's
called a methyl radical.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.932
So this is called--

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.234
that's called a free radical.

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:03.176
Or in this case, it's
methyl, because that's

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:04.477
what the chemistry is.

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:05.812
It's a methyl radical.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:09.682
As we'll see, you can make
radicals out of lots of stuff.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:11.884
Just take a hydrogen
and an electron

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.687
that have a free
unpaired electron left,

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.822
and it's a radical.

00:04:16.822 --> 00:04:24.664
I could start with ethane and
make an ethyl radical, C2H5

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:25.865
with a dot.

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:29.736
That dot is the
unpaired electron.

00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:31.638
And so on, and so on.

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:35.275
And so if you have free
radicals and they come along,

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:36.709
and there's some
other molecules--

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:38.711
it's not going to be this
one, but maybe there's

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:41.581
something like an antioxidant
in those blueberries.

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:43.182
It's going to come along.

00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:45.551
I've got electrons
I could give you.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:46.286
Here, have one.

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:47.620
I see how needy you are.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.355
I see how badly you want one.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:50.423
You can take one of mine.

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:51.724
And I'll be fine.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:54.560
I'll be fine.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.263
Or maybe we'll form
a bond with that.

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:59.532
Those are antioxidants.

00:04:59.532 --> 00:05:00.600
But that's not the topic.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.870
The topic is we're making this
new material called a polymer.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:04.871
So how does that work?

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:06.339
Well, to understand
that, we've got

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:09.275
to make a radical out
of a special case.

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:12.645
We've got to make a radical out
of a double-bonded molecule.

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:18.851
So now I'm going from methane
to the double-bonded ethane.

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:19.385
Now, hang on.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:22.722
Let me put this up here.

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:28.361
And in the case of a double
bond, something very important

00:05:28.361 --> 00:05:29.662
happens.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.265
Something very
important happens.

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:35.601
And the reason is that
I've got two bonds.

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.036
I've got two bonds, not one.

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:38.938
Well, that's what double bond--

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.774
that is what double bond means.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:44.110
But I can now-- because
I've got a double bond,

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:45.912
I can do something
very important.

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:47.347
I can do something
very important.

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:50.917
When I turn that
molecule into a radical,

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:53.119
something special happens.

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:55.688
So what I'm going to do is I'm
not going to call it chlorine.

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.657
I'm going to just call it R dot.

00:05:57.657 --> 00:06:01.060
And because now we're making a
polymer, and this is called--

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.328
well, it's got different names.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.664
It's called radical,
or sometimes you'll

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:10.002
see it as addition
polymerization.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:15.708
And this is the first kind of
polymer I want to talk about.

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:17.176
And so I'm going--

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:18.811
you saw how it
worked with chlorine.

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:21.247
I'm just going to say, I've
got something called R.

00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:23.850
And it's called a
radical initiator.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.953
And I'm going to draw
the dot there, just

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:28.621
like I had the dot on chlorine.

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:31.758
This is going to be
the radical initiator.

00:06:31.758 --> 00:06:35.428
Radical initiator.

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:40.666
And I'm going to now bring this
to my double-bonded molecule.

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:41.901
That's the key.

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:43.503
The key is in the double bond.

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:44.704
So watch what happens.

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:46.739
So first, here's what happens.

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:50.409
I've got R with its dot plus--

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:52.078
and again, I'm
going to go Lewis.

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:55.515
I'm going to have all those
electrons in the bonds spelled

00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:57.216
out for you with dots.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:07:02.455
So R plus C. Two pairs
of electrons in there.

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:03.122
Do you see that?

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.892
There's my double bond.

00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:07.093
And here are the hydrogens.

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:08.795
I'm not going to
draw them every time.

00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:10.897
Those sticks have
hydrogens on the ends.

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:14.934
But now watch what happens.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:19.272
So now, this radical,
like the chlorine,

00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:24.010
it sees this electron, and
it says, aha, I can take you.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:24.677
I can take you.

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:30.349
And I will be happy
if I take this.

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:34.153
And this double-bonded
molecule can't really

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:35.555
stop it from happening.

00:07:35.555 --> 00:07:40.827
So the radical now can bond
to the carbon over here.

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:42.528
It's got these two.

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:45.765
Those are those two
hydrogens that were there.

00:07:45.765 --> 00:07:46.866
But now watch what happens.

00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:51.337
Now I've got here, here, here.

00:07:51.337 --> 00:07:53.172
And then I've got this.

00:07:53.172 --> 00:07:54.073
And I've got this.

00:07:57.443 --> 00:08:00.246
But now this is a
radical, because this

00:08:00.246 --> 00:08:03.549
has an unpaired electron.

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:06.519
This has an unpaired electron.

00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:10.389
And so often what we'll
do is we'll write this.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:11.624
We'll often just write it.

00:08:11.624 --> 00:08:14.260
I'm just going to rewrite it,
just because then it gets us

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:19.298
in the mood for what's going
to happen next as a single bond

00:08:19.298 --> 00:08:22.468
with the two hydrogens and
the radical sitting there.

00:08:22.468 --> 00:08:24.337
I just took this
unpaired electron

00:08:24.337 --> 00:08:27.740
and put it out on the end there.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:30.676
That's a radical again.

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:33.846
And I haven't broken the bond.

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:35.681
That's the key.

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:37.783
The double bond
allowed a radical

00:08:37.783 --> 00:08:41.654
to come in, take
an electron, make

00:08:41.654 --> 00:08:45.224
a new radical out of the whole
thing, and remain stable.

00:08:45.224 --> 00:08:47.260
That's what the
double bond gave me.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:51.163
So if I go to step
two, what happens?

00:08:51.163 --> 00:08:53.966
If I go to step
two, well, now, I've

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:56.936
got this R that's sitting here.

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:59.972
And I've got these here.

00:08:59.972 --> 00:09:00.873
And I've got--

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.809
I'll put them in
explicitly, and there.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:12.285
Obviously, this is in a bucket
of these C2H4 molecules.

00:09:12.285 --> 00:09:14.921
They're all over the place.

00:09:14.921 --> 00:09:18.257
And now this is going to see--

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:20.426
so we're going to
do that, and this

00:09:20.426 --> 00:09:22.028
is going to see another one.

00:09:22.028 --> 00:09:23.663
Let's put that one in here.

00:09:23.663 --> 00:09:24.597
Here we go.

00:09:24.597 --> 00:09:27.700
A double bond, nice and
happy, floating along.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:32.204
And this radical
version of it sees it.

00:09:32.204 --> 00:09:34.206
And it says, nope, I
need that electron.

00:09:34.206 --> 00:09:35.841
Thank you very much.

00:09:35.841 --> 00:09:39.378
And so you get this.

00:09:39.378 --> 00:09:42.582
Single bond, single bond.

00:09:42.582 --> 00:09:46.385
And we'll just put it out here.

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:48.621
And so now, how many did I have?

00:09:48.621 --> 00:09:52.124
2, 4, and here's another one.

00:09:52.124 --> 00:09:53.626
And there it is.

00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:56.095
And I've got another radical.

00:09:56.095 --> 00:09:59.432
I've got another radical.

00:09:59.432 --> 00:10:04.470
The thing stays radical
until it finds another R dot.

00:10:08.407 --> 00:10:11.477
So this just goes
on and on and on.

00:10:11.477 --> 00:10:14.347
This is a new radical.

00:10:14.347 --> 00:10:17.550
New radical.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:23.823
And each time it finds a
double-bonded C2H4 molecule,

00:10:23.823 --> 00:10:27.627
it can take it, add it to the
chain, add it to the chain.

00:10:27.627 --> 00:10:30.296
Sometimes this is called
chain polymerization,

00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:33.165
addition polymerization,
radical polymerization.

00:10:33.165 --> 00:10:34.233
You see it's all the same.

00:10:34.233 --> 00:10:37.370
You see how it works,
because I'm making a chain,

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.272
and I keep on adding
the same building

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:42.141
block, the same molecule.

00:10:42.141 --> 00:10:44.844
That's really important.

00:10:44.844 --> 00:10:51.851
The same molecule, and
then et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:51.851 --> 00:10:54.754
Well, how long is et cetera?

00:10:54.754 --> 00:11:00.993
Well, typical polymers
go on for a long time.

00:11:00.993 --> 00:11:03.729
We're talking a
minimum of 100 times.

00:11:03.729 --> 00:11:07.900
100, you might be able to
draw that all the way down,

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.235
maybe down to there.

00:11:09.235 --> 00:11:13.873
But a lot of polymers go to
100,000 or a million chain

00:11:13.873 --> 00:11:16.208
links, a million.

00:11:16.208 --> 00:11:22.882
That makes this class of
materials extremely unique.

00:11:22.882 --> 00:11:25.518
And we're going to talk
about them in the next--

00:11:25.518 --> 00:11:29.255
over the next-- well,
during this week.

00:11:29.255 --> 00:11:31.090
What I want to do
is give you a couple

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:34.627
of reasons why they're
unique, and then we're

00:11:34.627 --> 00:11:37.129
going to go into my
"Why This Matters."

00:11:39.665 --> 00:11:41.567
What we started
with was a monomer.

00:11:41.567 --> 00:11:45.371
In this case, we started
with ethylene or ethene,

00:11:45.371 --> 00:11:48.708
and so here was our monomer.

00:11:48.708 --> 00:11:51.644
And what we ended up
with is a polymer.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.083
And in this case, it's--

00:11:57.083 --> 00:12:02.521
oh, boy-- I'll draw it
straight like I did there.

00:12:02.521 --> 00:12:03.723
OK, here we go.

00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:04.857
Here we go.

00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:07.259
This is the polymer.

00:12:07.259 --> 00:12:13.899
But see, I don't want to
write 100,000 carbon letters

00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:15.301
on my page.

00:12:15.301 --> 00:12:18.237
You can you can't blame
polymer chemists for that.

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:22.274
And so what we do is we
have a notation, where

00:12:22.274 --> 00:12:26.245
if this was the monomer, one.

00:12:26.245 --> 00:12:28.180
Poly, many.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:32.084
Mer, C2H4.

00:12:32.084 --> 00:12:34.520
Then what you do is
you take the monomer

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:36.122
and you write it
in a special way

00:12:36.122 --> 00:12:37.623
to show that it's
become a polymer.

00:12:37.623 --> 00:12:40.860
And you put the monomer inside.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.696
Oh, let's just write those
hydrogens explicitly.

00:12:43.696 --> 00:12:44.230
Why not?

00:12:46.665 --> 00:12:47.299
There they are.

00:12:47.299 --> 00:12:50.536
They were there, too.

00:12:50.536 --> 00:12:53.305
But now what you do is
you put parentheses.

00:12:53.305 --> 00:13:00.913
And to show that it's a polymer,
this bond comes out the edge.

00:13:00.913 --> 00:13:04.650
That shows I had this monomer.

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:09.188
Now, notice, this is the same
as a monomer, but a single bond.

00:13:09.188 --> 00:13:09.688
Why?

00:13:09.688 --> 00:13:13.659
Because that double bond, to
keep on making it radical,

00:13:13.659 --> 00:13:15.427
I change them all
into single bonds.

00:13:15.427 --> 00:13:18.731
Each time I made
the chain longer.

00:13:18.731 --> 00:13:23.435
And then we put an n, because
it could repeat n times.

00:13:23.435 --> 00:13:25.404
And that's a polymer.

00:13:25.404 --> 00:13:28.541
So the chain bonds are
sticking out of the edges

00:13:28.541 --> 00:13:31.277
like that of the parentheses.

00:13:31.277 --> 00:13:33.312
Polymer chemists aren't as--

00:13:33.312 --> 00:13:35.347
the crystallographers,
you don't want to upset.

00:13:35.347 --> 00:13:37.449
Polymer chemists are fine.

00:13:37.449 --> 00:13:41.987
So if you put brackets there,
yeah, they're OK with it.

00:13:41.987 --> 00:13:43.355
It's not so bad.

00:13:43.355 --> 00:13:45.090
Parentheses, brackets.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:48.494
The main thing is those
bonds that are polymerizing,

00:13:48.494 --> 00:13:50.696
those bonds that are
forming the chain

00:13:50.696 --> 00:13:52.965
have to come out
of the parentheses,

00:13:52.965 --> 00:13:56.936
and then the little n
there is how many times.

00:13:56.936 --> 00:13:59.772
So a couple of things
we can talk about.

00:13:59.772 --> 00:14:04.176
So polymer, monomer.

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:06.345
Well, one thing, a
couple of things.

00:14:06.345 --> 00:14:10.349
So the molecular weight
is going to be high.

00:14:10.349 --> 00:14:13.285
It's going to be
typically, and again,

00:14:13.285 --> 00:14:18.457
thousands of grams
per mole, because it's

00:14:18.457 --> 00:14:20.492
a mole of the molecule.

00:14:20.492 --> 00:14:22.528
This is a molecule.

00:14:22.528 --> 00:14:23.329
This is a molecule.

00:14:23.329 --> 00:14:28.167
It's just a very, very
long chain molecule.

00:14:28.167 --> 00:14:30.603
And so polymer people
and bio people,

00:14:30.603 --> 00:14:34.173
they like using Daltons.

00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:42.448
1 Dalton equals 1 gram per mole.

00:14:42.448 --> 00:14:44.483
Why switch to Daltons?

00:14:44.483 --> 00:14:47.253
I don't know!

00:14:47.253 --> 00:14:49.321
It's just a unit thing.

00:14:49.321 --> 00:14:50.656
So don't get confused.

00:14:50.656 --> 00:14:52.057
Dalton is a gram per mole.

00:14:52.057 --> 00:14:57.029
A kiloDalton is
1,000 grams per mole.

00:14:57.029 --> 00:15:00.499
We can say like a Da.

00:15:00.499 --> 00:15:04.570
Maybe they just
like the symbol, Da.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.071
Or kDa for kiloDaltons.

00:15:06.071 --> 00:15:08.240
It's just grams per
mole, thousands of grams.

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:14.079
But now one long chain, if
I know the molecular weight

00:15:14.079 --> 00:15:15.681
in grams per mole,
then I kind of also

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:18.250
could know how long it is.

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:20.319
Back and forth, it's
just a molecule.

00:15:20.319 --> 00:15:23.822
It's just that this is a
very, very big molecule.

00:15:23.822 --> 00:15:27.159
And so sometimes you'll see
it called a macromolecule.

00:15:27.159 --> 00:15:28.761
Macro.

00:15:28.761 --> 00:15:29.428
It's huge!

00:15:32.631 --> 00:15:36.535
It's a long chain.

00:15:36.535 --> 00:15:43.542
You just think about how the
length is on the order of--

00:15:43.542 --> 00:15:44.710
I'll spell it out-- microns.

00:15:47.746 --> 00:15:48.814
Just think about it.

00:15:48.814 --> 00:15:50.382
We've been talking about a
lot of different materials

00:15:50.382 --> 00:15:51.050
in this class.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:55.587
We've been talking about a lot
of different solid materials.

00:15:55.587 --> 00:15:56.622
Perfect crystals.

00:15:56.622 --> 00:15:58.324
Crystals with defects.

00:15:58.324 --> 00:16:00.259
Amorphous materials.

00:16:00.259 --> 00:16:02.227
Molecular solids.

00:16:02.227 --> 00:16:05.164
This is another class of
solid, but you can really

00:16:05.164 --> 00:16:07.066
start to think about why
this is so different.

00:16:07.066 --> 00:16:13.172
Because if this thing is
100,000 or a million units long,

00:16:13.172 --> 00:16:18.110
that strand is a very
long piece of spaghetti.

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:20.479
And that means that
different things can happen.

00:16:20.479 --> 00:16:24.850
And that's one of the reasons
why polymers are so interesting

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:27.586
and can be so useful.

00:16:27.586 --> 00:16:30.289
And so if you think about taking
it longer, this isn't even--

00:16:30.289 --> 00:16:32.057
we're not even
close to how long--

00:16:32.057 --> 00:16:34.626
this is still just a little
longer, but what I'm showing

00:16:34.626 --> 00:16:35.761
you is something important.

00:16:35.761 --> 00:16:38.330
If I've got spaghetti
that that's long,

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.200
you better believe
it can tangle up.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:43.202
And so when you think
about one of these strands

00:16:43.202 --> 00:16:44.603
or maybe a couple
of them, they're

00:16:44.603 --> 00:16:47.639
going to usually,
often, not always--

00:16:47.639 --> 00:16:50.576
you can make crystals out of
polymers, but a lot of times

00:16:50.576 --> 00:16:55.347
they're going to tangle,
because they're just so long.

00:16:55.347 --> 00:16:58.317
And the degree of the
length, by the way, that's

00:16:58.317 --> 00:17:04.323
another thing, degree
of polymerization.

00:17:04.323 --> 00:17:06.492
Ah, why was my O so big there?

00:17:06.492 --> 00:17:08.660
I don't know.

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:10.429
It was a moment.

00:17:10.429 --> 00:17:16.335
I'm feeling it with the O.
And that's the number of mer--

00:17:16.335 --> 00:17:18.203
yeah, it's a polymer--

00:17:18.203 --> 00:17:21.006
mer units.

00:17:21.006 --> 00:17:24.009
And that's per polymer.

00:17:24.009 --> 00:17:36.255
And this is average, because
as you can imagine, it's very--

00:17:36.255 --> 00:17:38.123
I'm going out to a million.

00:17:38.123 --> 00:17:42.761
Did I hit a million
or did I get 990,000?

00:17:42.761 --> 00:17:46.799
Or did I get a million and 10?

00:17:46.799 --> 00:17:49.935
You don't control
the length exactly.

00:17:49.935 --> 00:17:52.538
And so the degree
of polymerization

00:17:52.538 --> 00:17:55.340
is the number that
is the average.

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:56.308
It's the average.

00:17:56.308 --> 00:17:58.444
It's the same as the
molecular weight.

00:17:58.444 --> 00:18:01.280
It's an average, because the
strands have a distribution.

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:05.184
And we'll talk about
that more on Wednesday.

00:18:05.184 --> 00:18:09.021
But now I just want to get
down some polymer insights.

00:18:11.657 --> 00:18:13.292
So we got things like
molecular weight,

00:18:13.292 --> 00:18:17.129
degree of polymerization.

00:18:17.129 --> 00:18:19.965
Now, these are very long.

00:18:19.965 --> 00:18:23.335
And so you can imagine that
it's kind of like glasses

00:18:23.335 --> 00:18:24.269
when we did glasses.

00:18:24.269 --> 00:18:25.771
These are really long chains.

00:18:25.771 --> 00:18:29.174
You can imagine as a liquid
that might be pretty viscous.

00:18:29.174 --> 00:18:31.343
That might be pretty viscous.

00:18:31.343 --> 00:18:33.445
You could also imagine
that when you solidify it,

00:18:33.445 --> 00:18:36.949
it might be pretty disordered,
or maybe crystalline.

00:18:36.949 --> 00:18:38.717
It might have some
similarities with glass.

00:18:38.717 --> 00:18:40.652
Again, we'll talk about
that more Wednesday.

00:18:40.652 --> 00:18:43.088
But the one thing I
want to highlight now

00:18:43.088 --> 00:18:47.159
is that what can happen
because it's so long

00:18:47.159 --> 00:18:49.695
is that you can
literally get what?

00:18:49.695 --> 00:18:54.466
You can literally get like
one polymer chain that

00:18:54.466 --> 00:18:58.403
finds itself wanting to
form a crystal literally

00:18:58.403 --> 00:19:00.706
like in the middle of itself.

00:19:00.706 --> 00:19:03.609
And then it's like, OK, I can
stack and form a nice crystal,

00:19:03.609 --> 00:19:05.344
but now I can't again.

00:19:05.344 --> 00:19:10.382
And now, oh, look, I think I
can pack in nicely as a crystal.

00:19:10.382 --> 00:19:12.417
And now I can't again.

00:19:12.417 --> 00:19:14.520
And it just goes, I could--

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:16.622
I'm really enjoying this.

00:19:16.622 --> 00:19:18.190
I could just keep going.

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:21.326
That's one polymer strand!

00:19:21.326 --> 00:19:22.761
And it would keep going.

00:19:22.761 --> 00:19:27.499
It literally would, because
it's like 100,000 units long.

00:19:27.499 --> 00:19:30.235
So you can imagine
that polymers, just one

00:19:30.235 --> 00:19:33.772
strand of a polymer can
be incredibly complicated,

00:19:33.772 --> 00:19:37.442
because it might have some
regions that are crystalline

00:19:37.442 --> 00:19:38.610
and some that are amorphous.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:42.114
And by the way, we know
that the crystalline region

00:19:42.114 --> 00:19:43.882
will be very different.

00:19:43.882 --> 00:19:46.418
This is going to have the
lowest volume per mole.

00:19:46.418 --> 00:19:48.554
So this will have-- maybe
this will be the hardest.

00:19:48.554 --> 00:19:51.590
Maybe it will be
the most brittle.

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:53.392
So does it crystallize?

00:19:53.392 --> 00:19:54.293
Does it not?

00:19:54.293 --> 00:19:57.729
That's going to be something
that's very important.

00:19:57.729 --> 00:20:00.632
How much of it crystallizes?

00:20:00.632 --> 00:20:03.602
A micron, by the way, the
length is also important.

00:20:03.602 --> 00:20:06.538
That's like visible
light, wavelength.

00:20:06.538 --> 00:20:10.542
So optical properties will
depend on these things.

00:20:10.542 --> 00:20:16.048
And so what you have is
this new form of matter

00:20:16.048 --> 00:20:18.917
that we have in
the last 50 years

00:20:18.917 --> 00:20:22.688
engineered and understood
more and more how to control.

00:20:22.688 --> 00:20:28.994
And so we're talking about
this addition polymerization.

00:20:28.994 --> 00:20:34.666
This list goes on and
on and on now today.

00:20:34.666 --> 00:20:36.134
So what is this list?

00:20:36.134 --> 00:20:37.569
Well, these are all--

00:20:37.569 --> 00:20:39.204
I want you to notice here--

00:20:39.204 --> 00:20:41.073
monomer.

00:20:41.073 --> 00:20:42.874
This is the polymer name.

00:20:42.874 --> 00:20:44.610
This is the monomer.

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:45.444
Here's the polymer.

00:20:45.444 --> 00:20:46.845
They should have put
parentheses there.

00:20:46.845 --> 00:20:48.347
We'll excuse that,
because you can

00:20:48.347 --> 00:20:51.383
feel that those are going
outside of the parentheses

00:20:51.383 --> 00:20:53.485
or brackets.

00:20:53.485 --> 00:20:55.087
And then here's the use.

00:20:55.087 --> 00:20:56.221
So what did we just do?

00:20:56.221 --> 00:20:58.023
We just did polyethylene.

00:20:58.023 --> 00:20:59.858
Polyethylene is right there.

00:20:59.858 --> 00:21:01.693
That's the polymer polyethylene.

00:21:01.693 --> 00:21:03.795
There is the monomer.

00:21:03.795 --> 00:21:04.896
There is the polymer.

00:21:04.896 --> 00:21:06.732
Now, notice something
about every single one

00:21:06.732 --> 00:21:08.200
of these monomers.

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:08.867
There it is.

00:21:08.867 --> 00:21:10.435
They've all got
that double bond,

00:21:10.435 --> 00:21:12.904
that double-bonded carbon.

00:21:12.904 --> 00:21:15.641
So when I put a
radical initiator

00:21:15.641 --> 00:21:22.514
in a soup of those monomers,
I can form polymer chains.

00:21:22.514 --> 00:21:23.982
I can form polymer chains.

00:21:23.982 --> 00:21:25.817
And those are the
polymers you make.

00:21:25.817 --> 00:21:27.419
And notice they all start--

00:21:27.419 --> 00:21:29.521
these are the same
unit repeating

00:21:29.521 --> 00:21:31.189
over and over and over again.

00:21:31.189 --> 00:21:35.093
And notice the flexibility here.

00:21:35.093 --> 00:21:38.096
So in polyethylene, which
is one of the simplest cases

00:21:38.096 --> 00:21:41.967
and is the most common polymer,
these are just a few uses.

00:21:41.967 --> 00:21:45.070
If I just take one of
those hydrogens off

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.939
of one of the two carbons--

00:21:47.939 --> 00:21:50.108
there's polyethylene up there--

00:21:50.108 --> 00:21:52.711
if I just take one
of the hydrogens off,

00:21:52.711 --> 00:21:58.183
and instead I put
a benzene ring.

00:21:58.183 --> 00:22:00.652
So now I've got this.

00:22:03.188 --> 00:22:03.922
Oh, boy.

00:22:03.922 --> 00:22:05.090
Did I draw that?

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:05.957
Yeah, it's OK.

00:22:05.957 --> 00:22:08.460
Here's my hydrogens
coming off of the benzene.

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:09.728
There you go.

00:22:09.728 --> 00:22:10.929
There you go.

00:22:10.929 --> 00:22:15.467
And there, and
there, and oh, good.

00:22:15.467 --> 00:22:19.838
I've drawn
polystyrene, haven't I?

00:22:19.838 --> 00:22:23.241
No, I have not!

00:22:23.241 --> 00:22:24.676
Because you've got to come out!

00:22:27.779 --> 00:22:29.715
That's a polymer.

00:22:29.715 --> 00:22:30.982
Now it's a polymer.

00:22:30.982 --> 00:22:31.783
I came out.

00:22:35.954 --> 00:22:37.155
Well, that's pretty simple.

00:22:37.155 --> 00:22:40.659
All I did is I just
took the polyethylene,

00:22:40.659 --> 00:22:42.361
and I put a benzene ring there.

00:22:42.361 --> 00:22:45.130
And I get Styrofoam if
I put air through it

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:47.065
as I make it processing.

00:22:47.065 --> 00:22:49.634
Or I can make all sorts
of other things out of it.

00:22:49.634 --> 00:22:51.770
That's polystyrene.

00:22:51.770 --> 00:22:55.173
That's polystyrene.

00:22:55.173 --> 00:23:00.946
This is why polymers have
become such an incredibly

00:23:00.946 --> 00:23:05.150
important and enormous
part of our lives,

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:08.920
because you can see if I just
change the monomer, I just

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:11.189
change one thing
in one molecule,

00:23:11.189 --> 00:23:13.492
and then get a bucket of those.

00:23:13.492 --> 00:23:18.430
I might completely change the
solid that comes out of it.

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.265
But all sorts of things
are going to change.

00:23:20.265 --> 00:23:22.667
You can imagine that just
the way the chain folds up

00:23:22.667 --> 00:23:25.203
on itself like that,
maybe the way the chain

00:23:25.203 --> 00:23:28.473
comes and talks to
another chain, or maybe

00:23:28.473 --> 00:23:29.975
a part of itself.

00:23:29.975 --> 00:23:34.546
When I say talk and I'm talking
about chemistry, I mean bond.

00:23:34.546 --> 00:23:36.581
Is it Van der Waals?

00:23:36.581 --> 00:23:40.018
The surface area
is enormous here.

00:23:40.018 --> 00:23:41.653
The surface area of one mol--

00:23:41.653 --> 00:23:42.487
we did this, right?

00:23:42.487 --> 00:23:43.655
We looked at boiling points.

00:23:43.655 --> 00:23:48.326
As you get a little longer,
a little longer, to C12.

00:23:48.326 --> 00:23:51.630
This is C hundreds of thousands.

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:57.536
So the amount of bonding,
London dispersion, is enormous.

00:23:57.536 --> 00:23:59.671
That's one of the reasons
these can be very strong.

00:23:59.671 --> 00:24:01.440
But the tunability
is also enormous,

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.942
because I can just swap
in a different group

00:24:03.942 --> 00:24:05.744
here, and polymerize that.

00:24:09.815 --> 00:24:12.684
This is why this was so
exciting when it was--

00:24:12.684 --> 00:24:16.288
polymers were first made
really in the '40s and '50s.

00:24:16.288 --> 00:24:21.126
They really started coming
into the market in the '70s

00:24:21.126 --> 00:24:22.294
en masse.

00:24:22.294 --> 00:24:27.632
And now I want to tell
you why this matters.

00:24:27.632 --> 00:24:30.268
We're going to start
with a commercial.

00:24:30.268 --> 00:24:38.310
This is an ad on television
by Pepsi in 1978.

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:39.978
And I thought this
would be a nice way

00:24:39.978 --> 00:24:41.646
to start my "Why This Matters."

00:24:41.646 --> 00:24:43.882
It's only 30 seconds.

00:24:43.882 --> 00:24:44.382
Uh-oh.

00:24:47.819 --> 00:24:48.820
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:51.456
Pepsi Cola's new 2
liter plastic bottle.

00:24:56.428 --> 00:24:57.429
It's tough.

00:25:03.435 --> 00:25:04.936
Really tough.

00:25:09.808 --> 00:25:13.111
And besides being touch,
Pepsi Cola's new 2 liter

00:25:13.111 --> 00:25:16.414
plastic bottle is 25%
lighter than glass.

00:25:16.414 --> 00:25:17.682
It's tough and light.

00:25:17.682 --> 00:25:18.250
[END PLAYBACK]

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:19.017
Tough and light.

00:25:19.017 --> 00:25:22.821
Now, apart from some other
potential differences

00:25:22.821 --> 00:25:26.558
you may have noticed
with today's times, that

00:25:26.558 --> 00:25:27.225
was the key.

00:25:27.225 --> 00:25:29.661
That was one of the keys.

00:25:29.661 --> 00:25:31.396
So they could make--

00:25:31.396 --> 00:25:34.199
Pepsi and Coke,
Coca-Cola, could make

00:25:34.199 --> 00:25:38.169
bottles that were lighter
than glass, but not break.

00:25:38.169 --> 00:25:39.971
That was a very big deal.

00:25:39.971 --> 00:25:42.674
That was a very big deal.

00:25:42.674 --> 00:25:44.776
And they really started--

00:25:44.776 --> 00:25:48.079
in a lot of ways, they
started the revolution

00:25:48.079 --> 00:25:52.517
of plastics and polymers
in commercial products.

00:25:56.221 --> 00:25:59.658
Now, if you fast
forward to today,

00:25:59.658 --> 00:26:01.560
I just want to show you
how bottles are made.

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:02.894
That's a 2 liter bottle.

00:26:02.894 --> 00:26:05.463
Here is a 2 liter
bottle making plant.

00:26:05.463 --> 00:26:07.799
And I think it's always
interesting to see how what

00:26:07.799 --> 00:26:09.334
you're buying is actually made.

00:26:09.334 --> 00:26:11.236
So here's just a
little short video.

00:26:11.236 --> 00:26:11.903
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:26:11.903 --> 00:26:14.973
Harden almost instantly thanks
to a built-in cooling system.

00:26:17.609 --> 00:26:19.644
These preforms are
now on their way

00:26:19.644 --> 00:26:21.646
to becoming single-serving
juice bottles.

00:26:27.052 --> 00:26:30.188
This is another plastic
injection molding machine.

00:26:30.188 --> 00:26:32.657
It uses the same
method to make preforms

00:26:32.657 --> 00:26:35.594
for a different model, 1
and 1/2 to 2 liter bottles.

00:26:47.205 --> 00:26:50.842
The preform's next stop is a
machine called a reheat stretch

00:26:50.842 --> 00:26:51.343
blow molder.

00:26:53.945 --> 00:26:57.882
In a matter of seconds, it
heats each preform just enough

00:26:57.882 --> 00:27:01.486
to make the plastic
malleable, then inserts a rod

00:27:01.486 --> 00:27:04.155
to stretch the
preform lengthwise,

00:27:04.155 --> 00:27:07.392
while at the same time
blowing in air at extremely

00:27:07.392 --> 00:27:09.361
high pressure.

00:27:09.361 --> 00:27:13.031
This forces the preform
into a bottle-shaped mold.

00:27:13.031 --> 00:27:15.667
Cold water circulates
within the mold to cool.

00:27:15.667 --> 00:27:16.635
[END PLAYBACK]

00:27:16.635 --> 00:27:18.069
So the reason I
wanted to show you

00:27:18.069 --> 00:27:23.008
this is it just gives you a
sense of the pace, the pace.

00:27:23.008 --> 00:27:27.412
Now, the pace is connected to
the material and the chemistry.

00:27:27.412 --> 00:27:31.282
And it's things that
you already know.

00:27:31.282 --> 00:27:33.885
When we did stress
strain curves,

00:27:33.885 --> 00:27:38.056
one of the whole regions of it
is called plastic deformation.

00:27:38.056 --> 00:27:40.892
These are plastics.

00:27:40.892 --> 00:27:42.961
So one of the big
advantages of these things

00:27:42.961 --> 00:27:46.031
is you can make like a small
mold like you do first,

00:27:46.031 --> 00:27:49.634
and then that can be doled
out to different processes.

00:27:49.634 --> 00:27:53.304
And in a matter of less than
a second with a little heat,

00:27:53.304 --> 00:27:56.374
a little pressure, it can be
made into any bottle you want,

00:27:56.374 --> 00:27:58.043
and then the next
one, and the next one.

00:28:00.879 --> 00:28:02.981
Well, let's see.

00:28:02.981 --> 00:28:06.117
We buy-- I'm going to write
a couple of things here--

00:28:06.117 --> 00:28:06.618
let's see.

00:28:06.618 --> 00:28:14.659
We buy as humans 1
million of those bottles,

00:28:14.659 --> 00:28:15.360
plastic bottles--

00:28:19.664 --> 00:28:21.032
anybody want to give me a unit?

00:28:21.032 --> 00:28:22.400
Day.

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:24.202
It's per minute.

00:28:24.202 --> 00:28:26.371
[GASPING]

00:28:26.371 --> 00:28:28.106
We buy a million
globally per minute.

00:28:30.108 --> 00:28:32.544
And the thing is, if you're
talking about plastic bottles,

00:28:32.544 --> 00:28:35.346
91% of them are not recycled.

00:28:35.346 --> 00:28:38.683
91% of them are not recycled.

00:28:38.683 --> 00:28:42.721
And so my "Why
This Matters," it's

00:28:42.721 --> 00:28:44.322
going to turn a
little dark right now,

00:28:44.322 --> 00:28:52.297
because the thing is, if you
compare with what nature does,

00:28:52.297 --> 00:28:53.531
these are nature's polymers.

00:28:53.531 --> 00:28:56.835
And I will talk about them
on Friday a little bit more.

00:28:56.835 --> 00:28:58.670
And you can say, well,
nature's had millions

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:00.004
of years to work on this, sure.

00:29:00.004 --> 00:29:04.676
Nature is the world's
greatest polymer engineer.

00:29:04.676 --> 00:29:06.678
We are all polymers.

00:29:06.678 --> 00:29:10.281
We are all polymers
of different types.

00:29:10.281 --> 00:29:15.553
Amino acids, sugars,
cellulose, keratin.

00:29:15.553 --> 00:29:18.223
Nature does this
in so many ways.

00:29:18.223 --> 00:29:23.128
And of course, by definition,
these are biodegradable.

00:29:23.128 --> 00:29:24.362
Now, that's what nature makes.

00:29:24.362 --> 00:29:27.365
Here's what we make.

00:29:27.365 --> 00:29:30.001
Now, this is a beach.

00:29:30.001 --> 00:29:30.869
That's a coastline.

00:29:30.869 --> 00:29:33.204
And there's a lot of places
where you can get some data.

00:29:33.204 --> 00:29:34.939
I like Our World in Data.

00:29:34.939 --> 00:29:37.709
You guys can take a look at
that and many other resources.

00:29:37.709 --> 00:29:38.376
Here's a beach.

00:29:38.376 --> 00:29:39.644
Here is a guy.

00:29:39.644 --> 00:29:41.813
It's hard to see, but
he's actually in a boat.

00:29:41.813 --> 00:29:43.581
That's a boat.

00:29:43.581 --> 00:29:46.451
So that's all water.

00:29:46.451 --> 00:29:51.656
And of course, there's
a lot of information

00:29:51.656 --> 00:29:55.860
lately on things like
how much of this stuff

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:57.829
animals are eating.

00:29:57.829 --> 00:30:00.431
And there's also
a lot of pictures

00:30:00.431 --> 00:30:01.900
that are sometimes
difficult to see,

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:05.537
where animals get stuck in a
piece of plastic, for example.

00:30:05.537 --> 00:30:09.340
And that can lead to death,
or deformity, as in this case.

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:16.648
And the thing is that we
have to think very carefully

00:30:16.648 --> 00:30:17.949
about what we're doing.

00:30:17.949 --> 00:30:21.019
And we have not done that
with regard to polymers.

00:30:21.019 --> 00:30:23.288
We have not done that at all.

00:30:26.291 --> 00:30:29.460
And just to give you a couple
more numbers, here's a chart.

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:34.532
This is how polymers
are used today.

00:30:34.532 --> 00:30:36.367
Packaging is 40%.

00:30:36.367 --> 00:30:40.405
40% of plastic produced
today is in packaging.

00:30:40.405 --> 00:30:41.739
And the thing is--

00:30:41.739 --> 00:30:42.240
sorry.

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:46.044
40% of the plastics
are used only once,

00:30:46.044 --> 00:30:47.011
and then thrown away.

00:30:47.011 --> 00:30:48.012
We all know that.

00:30:48.012 --> 00:30:53.785
We drink a bottle of water out
of plastic, or all the way back

00:30:53.785 --> 00:30:58.156
to the '70s, the Pepsi and the
Coke, and we throw it away.

00:31:00.758 --> 00:31:02.994
But the thing is
that most of that,

00:31:02.994 --> 00:31:05.930
a lot of that, most of that
winds up in the oceans.

00:31:05.930 --> 00:31:09.200
And it takes about a half a
thousand years, 500 years,

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:11.102
to decompose.

00:31:11.102 --> 00:31:13.571
So let's see.

00:31:13.571 --> 00:31:15.106
A couple more numbers.

00:31:15.106 --> 00:31:22.881
So first of all, only 7%
of all plastic is recycled.

00:31:28.086 --> 00:31:32.357
Now, Coca-Cola and
Pepsi could use

00:31:32.357 --> 00:31:34.292
a lot more recycled plastic.

00:31:34.292 --> 00:31:36.527
One of the main reasons
they don't is the containers

00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:37.795
wouldn't be see through.

00:31:37.795 --> 00:31:39.030
And we can't have that, right?

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:41.733
As consumers, we've
got to see it.

00:31:41.733 --> 00:31:46.371
It's got to be this
beautiful, clear container.

00:31:46.371 --> 00:31:49.674
But as I said, most of
what's not recycled winds up

00:31:49.674 --> 00:31:51.042
in the oceans.

00:31:51.042 --> 00:31:56.114
And let's see, in 2050--

00:31:56.114 --> 00:31:57.415
here's one.

00:31:57.415 --> 00:32:04.889
In 2050, the
plastic in the ocean

00:32:04.889 --> 00:32:11.596
will equal the
weight of all fish.

00:32:16.801 --> 00:32:22.073
That includes whales and
large sea animals, everyone.

00:32:22.073 --> 00:32:25.209
Now, the thing is that--

00:32:25.209 --> 00:32:26.110
let's see.

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:28.780
Many of these plastics,
like polystyrene, there

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:32.283
is an example, they decompose.

00:32:32.283 --> 00:32:35.887
And so when they're
large, large,

00:32:35.887 --> 00:32:37.789
there is a chance
to collect them.

00:32:37.789 --> 00:32:41.326
But the sunlight out in the
ocean, they float up to the top

00:32:41.326 --> 00:32:43.428
or they get currents
that mix them,

00:32:43.428 --> 00:32:47.632
and the sunlight, the UV energy
from the sun, decomposes them.

00:32:47.632 --> 00:32:50.568
Eventually they get smaller
and smaller and smaller.

00:32:50.568 --> 00:32:53.004
And eventually, they
get really tiny.

00:32:53.004 --> 00:32:54.305
Those are called microplastics.

00:32:54.305 --> 00:32:55.606
You say, well, why is that bad?

00:32:55.606 --> 00:32:58.242
Because it's toxic,
for one thing.

00:32:58.242 --> 00:33:01.179
Polystyrene is toxic.

00:33:01.179 --> 00:33:03.948
The styrene molecule is toxic.

00:33:03.948 --> 00:33:07.618
It's a carcinogen.
It's a carcinogen.

00:33:07.618 --> 00:33:10.321
People who eat seafood
in some studies

00:33:10.321 --> 00:33:14.192
are estimated to be eating
11,000 pieces of microplastic

00:33:14.192 --> 00:33:15.727
a year.

00:33:15.727 --> 00:33:19.330
That's what they're
ingesting today.

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:22.567
So this is the problem.

00:33:22.567 --> 00:33:26.838
We're making these single-use
products, single-use, put it

00:33:26.838 --> 00:33:31.709
in recycling because I'm a good
citizen, but 9 out of 10 times

00:33:31.709 --> 00:33:34.278
it doesn't matter, because
it's not getting recycled.

00:33:34.278 --> 00:33:35.680
Oh, and then it
goes in the ocean

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:37.749
where it stays for 500 years.

00:33:37.749 --> 00:33:39.650
There's a plastic bottle.

00:33:39.650 --> 00:33:42.120
There's fishing lines.

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:43.721
So this is the problem.

00:33:43.721 --> 00:33:48.826
We should not be using things
on the minute timescale that

00:33:48.826 --> 00:33:52.030
then take a half thousand
years to decompose

00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:55.500
and are extremely
toxic when they do.

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:58.603
This is clearly a problem.

00:33:58.603 --> 00:34:02.607
Now, there are a lot of
people working on this

00:34:02.607 --> 00:34:04.208
and thinking about this.

00:34:04.208 --> 00:34:04.942
Not enough.

00:34:04.942 --> 00:34:07.111
And I want to tell
you about a book

00:34:07.111 --> 00:34:10.581
that I read when it came
out a long time ago.

00:34:10.581 --> 00:34:11.549
It's a wonderful book.

00:34:11.549 --> 00:34:16.587
And I got reintroduced
to it last week.

00:34:16.587 --> 00:34:18.556
By David McKay, who
unfortunately passed away

00:34:18.556 --> 00:34:21.192
recently, but he wrote this
book called Sustainable Energy

00:34:21.192 --> 00:34:23.161
Without the Hot Air.

00:34:23.161 --> 00:34:27.231
And I highly recommend you
read this if you haven't.

00:34:27.231 --> 00:34:27.799
It's free.

00:34:27.799 --> 00:34:28.866
It's a free download.

00:34:28.866 --> 00:34:30.368
WithoutHotAir.com.

00:34:30.368 --> 00:34:35.440
Because he just goes through
sort of simply the math.

00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:37.608
And the reason I'm bringing
this to your attention--

00:34:37.608 --> 00:34:39.177
he doesn't really
talk about plastics.

00:34:39.177 --> 00:34:40.244
He talks about energy--

00:34:40.244 --> 00:34:43.514
is I love this
discussion in chapter 19.

00:34:43.514 --> 00:34:47.685
Chapter 19 is titled
"Every BIG helps."

00:34:47.685 --> 00:34:49.954
He says, if everyone
does a little,

00:34:49.954 --> 00:34:53.224
we'll achieve only a little.

00:34:53.224 --> 00:34:54.058
That is true.

00:34:54.058 --> 00:34:58.629
We have this way of
talking about these crises

00:34:58.629 --> 00:35:00.631
in little bits, because
it makes us feel better,

00:35:00.631 --> 00:35:03.000
and it makes
companies feel better.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:05.303
You talk about this with
the plastics in straws

00:35:05.303 --> 00:35:08.439
has gotten a lot of press.

00:35:08.439 --> 00:35:12.343
And companies kind
of do their part.

00:35:12.343 --> 00:35:13.878
Although, Starbucks
is kind of funny,

00:35:13.878 --> 00:35:18.182
because they replaced all
plastic straws with paper

00:35:18.182 --> 00:35:20.585
straws encased in plastic.

00:35:20.585 --> 00:35:24.622
That's actually more than the
plastic straws to begin with.

00:35:24.622 --> 00:35:26.924
Single-use plastic again.

00:35:26.924 --> 00:35:28.059
But anyway, they're trying.

00:35:28.059 --> 00:35:28.693
They walk away.

00:35:28.693 --> 00:35:29.827
They say, we tried.

00:35:29.827 --> 00:35:31.762
No!

00:35:31.762 --> 00:35:33.998
Because that's doing a little.

00:35:33.998 --> 00:35:39.704
The plastic in the ocean
is 0.03% from straws.

00:35:39.704 --> 00:35:42.106
There's so much
more we have to do.

00:35:42.106 --> 00:35:44.675
We can't think little
about problems like this.

00:35:44.675 --> 00:35:47.678
We can't.

00:35:47.678 --> 00:35:49.981
We can't do that.

00:35:49.981 --> 00:35:51.682
Now, there are
some big projects.

00:35:51.682 --> 00:35:54.752
This is one that's
gotten some attention.

00:35:54.752 --> 00:35:56.387
This is called Ocean Cleanup.

00:35:56.387 --> 00:35:58.022
Now, they've gotten
a lot of attention.

00:35:58.022 --> 00:36:01.359
They've got these rigs out
there that could collect some

00:36:01.359 --> 00:36:04.262
of this plastic in the ocean.

00:36:04.262 --> 00:36:07.031
And it's at least thinking big.

00:36:07.031 --> 00:36:09.400
There's a lot of bold
claims that they're making.

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:12.703
A lot of bold claims,
a lot of hype.

00:36:12.703 --> 00:36:15.740
But at least it's an
attempt to do something big.

00:36:15.740 --> 00:36:17.408
One of the limitations
here though

00:36:17.408 --> 00:36:22.446
is this technology can't
go below a centimeter.

00:36:22.446 --> 00:36:23.414
It can't go below that.

00:36:23.414 --> 00:36:26.284
It's not going to collect
below a centimeter.

00:36:26.284 --> 00:36:28.219
But there are some
studies that estimate

00:36:28.219 --> 00:36:30.154
that 90% of the
plastic in the ocean

00:36:30.154 --> 00:36:33.558
already is below a
centimeter, and 60%,

00:36:33.558 --> 00:36:37.929
70% of that has already sunk.

00:36:37.929 --> 00:36:41.098
Talk about the need to think
about this in a big way.

00:36:41.098 --> 00:36:44.168
This is a very big problem.

00:36:44.168 --> 00:36:46.504
And I hope that
more and more people

00:36:46.504 --> 00:36:48.873
get excited about
trying to tackle it

00:36:48.873 --> 00:36:52.310
in big ways, not little ways.

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:56.280
That was my "Why This
Matters," nice and happy.

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:57.882
Nice and happy, why?

00:36:57.882 --> 00:37:00.885
Because we're going to
solve these problems.

00:37:00.885 --> 00:37:01.385
That's why.

00:37:01.385 --> 00:37:02.220
We have to.

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:05.056
We have no choice.

00:37:05.056 --> 00:37:07.058
I mentioned there were
two ways to make plastics

00:37:07.058 --> 00:37:08.192
that I want to talk about.

00:37:08.192 --> 00:37:09.994
I want to talk about
the second way now.

00:37:14.198 --> 00:37:18.002
So the first way is called
radical polymerization,

00:37:18.002 --> 00:37:21.872
because I take one
mer, and then I make it

00:37:21.872 --> 00:37:24.041
into a really long chain.

00:37:24.041 --> 00:37:29.080
The second way-- and that right
there is nylon, by the way.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:31.582
Nylon can't be made that way.

00:37:31.582 --> 00:37:33.417
Nylon can't be made that way.

00:37:33.417 --> 00:37:34.819
And here's the reason.

00:37:34.819 --> 00:37:38.356
Because in the second
way of making polymers,

00:37:38.356 --> 00:37:41.158
we take two different
types of mers.

00:37:41.158 --> 00:37:48.699
And this way is called
condensation polymerization.

00:37:59.310 --> 00:38:03.848
It's a different way of
forming very, very long chains.

00:38:03.848 --> 00:38:05.750
So on Wednesday,
we'll talk about what

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:07.118
you can do with
these chains, how

00:38:07.118 --> 00:38:09.086
you can modify the properties,
kind like we do with glass.

00:38:09.086 --> 00:38:10.655
We talked about the
fundamentals of glass,

00:38:10.655 --> 00:38:12.390
and then we talked
about engineering it.

00:38:12.390 --> 00:38:13.791
On Wednesday, we'll
talk about how

00:38:13.791 --> 00:38:17.695
to engineer the properties
of these polymers.

00:38:17.695 --> 00:38:20.564
But right now, I want to just
focus on how to make them.

00:38:20.564 --> 00:38:22.733
And so in condensation
polymerization,

00:38:22.733 --> 00:38:25.703
the goal is also to make
these super long strands,

00:38:25.703 --> 00:38:28.239
but you do it with two
different starting molecules

00:38:28.239 --> 00:38:30.508
and no initiator.

00:38:30.508 --> 00:38:32.043
So how does that work?

00:38:32.043 --> 00:38:34.278
Well, I'll take a--

00:38:34.278 --> 00:38:36.681
I'll get to nylon in a sec.

00:38:36.681 --> 00:38:38.182
But first, let's
just take something

00:38:38.182 --> 00:38:40.384
called the dicarboxylic acid.

00:38:40.384 --> 00:38:43.587
And what I'm going to do is I'm
going to leave a box inside.

00:38:43.587 --> 00:38:45.756
And I'm going to say
that could be something.

00:38:45.756 --> 00:38:46.924
That could be something.

00:38:46.924 --> 00:38:50.328
But I know that on that
something, I've got this.

00:38:50.328 --> 00:38:57.401
I've got a carbon, oxygen,
and hydrogen, and on this side

00:38:57.401 --> 00:38:58.536
I've got the same thing.

00:39:03.407 --> 00:39:05.109
OH.

00:39:05.109 --> 00:39:08.479
So I've got the same
thing on both sides.

00:39:08.479 --> 00:39:10.147
That's a dicarboxylic acid.

00:39:10.147 --> 00:39:12.616
It's carboxylic acid.

00:39:12.616 --> 00:39:17.488
Now, on this side, I'm
going to have another mer,

00:39:17.488 --> 00:39:19.156
and it's going to be different.

00:39:19.156 --> 00:39:22.560
And this one, I'm also
going to have a box.

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:23.627
And over here.

00:39:23.627 --> 00:39:24.195
[SNEEZING]

00:39:24.195 --> 00:39:25.930
Gesundheit.

00:39:25.930 --> 00:39:27.932
I've got NH2.

00:39:27.932 --> 00:39:31.435
And over here I've got NH2.

00:39:31.435 --> 00:39:31.936
NH2.

00:39:35.139 --> 00:39:38.209
Emphasizing the N is what's
connected to the box there.

00:39:38.209 --> 00:39:39.844
That's why I drew it that way.

00:39:39.844 --> 00:39:40.978
And that's called an amine.

00:39:40.978 --> 00:39:42.546
This is an amine.

00:39:42.546 --> 00:39:44.682
And this is called a diamine.

00:39:44.682 --> 00:39:47.051
So this is a diamine
dicarboxylic acid.

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:52.623
And what happens is when those
two molecules-- so the box

00:39:52.623 --> 00:39:53.424
can be anything.

00:39:55.993 --> 00:40:01.899
The box can be whatever for now.

00:40:05.736 --> 00:40:08.672
If it's got those ends
to it, if it's got

00:40:08.672 --> 00:40:11.242
the carboxylic acid and amine--

00:40:11.242 --> 00:40:12.643
so on the one hand,
the molecule's

00:40:12.643 --> 00:40:15.479
got these groups on the
end, and on the other hand,

00:40:15.479 --> 00:40:18.449
it's got these, then what
happens when I put them

00:40:18.449 --> 00:40:20.418
in solution together?

00:40:20.418 --> 00:40:24.188
Well, what happens is
these two pieces react.

00:40:24.188 --> 00:40:25.656
These two pieces react.

00:40:25.656 --> 00:40:29.960
And so what you get is--

00:40:29.960 --> 00:40:30.828
let's see.

00:40:30.828 --> 00:40:35.099
You've got C.
Here's my box there.

00:40:35.099 --> 00:40:38.135
There's the O down there.

00:40:38.135 --> 00:40:42.173
And let's see.

00:40:42.173 --> 00:40:44.642
Now here's the
carbon on this side.

00:40:44.642 --> 00:40:46.076
Oh, boy.

00:40:46.076 --> 00:40:48.412
I'm going to run out of
room, and I don't want to.

00:40:48.412 --> 00:40:52.616
So I'm going to move over here.

00:40:52.616 --> 00:40:59.657
And recycle these boards so
that I can draw it nice and big.

00:41:02.993 --> 00:41:07.798
So now what happens?

00:41:07.798 --> 00:41:08.732
So these are reacting.

00:41:15.105 --> 00:41:16.240
And so now what do I have?

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:22.713
So I have C, double bond O.
There's my carboxylic acid.

00:41:22.713 --> 00:41:24.548
There's the box from
the left-hand molecule,

00:41:24.548 --> 00:41:25.749
the one on the left there.

00:41:25.749 --> 00:41:28.953
Here's another C, double bond O.

00:41:28.953 --> 00:41:30.120
But look at what's happened.

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:33.657
I've formed a
carbon-nitrogen link.

00:41:33.657 --> 00:41:35.092
We'll talk about
that in a second.

00:41:35.092 --> 00:41:38.729
And then here's another box
that came from the amine.

00:41:38.729 --> 00:41:42.933
And this is an NH.

00:41:42.933 --> 00:41:45.402
And then there'd be
like another H there.

00:41:45.402 --> 00:41:47.137
And there'd be another H--

00:41:47.137 --> 00:41:48.506
yes, another OH here.

00:41:54.211 --> 00:41:56.313
Yeah.

00:41:56.313 --> 00:41:57.214
Yeah.

00:41:57.214 --> 00:41:57.882
NH2.

00:41:57.882 --> 00:41:59.283
So I got that back.

00:41:59.283 --> 00:42:00.251
And what's happened?

00:42:00.251 --> 00:42:03.187
What's happened is
I've made water.

00:42:03.187 --> 00:42:06.657
I've made water, plus I've
connected these two molecules

00:42:06.657 --> 00:42:07.157
together.

00:42:09.994 --> 00:42:12.663
So if I want to be
complete about it,

00:42:12.663 --> 00:42:14.031
I've got to add
a water molecule,

00:42:14.031 --> 00:42:16.734
because that's what had
happened in the middle here.

00:42:16.734 --> 00:42:21.071
The OH group saw the
H on the NH2 group,

00:42:21.071 --> 00:42:23.107
and it said, hey, I
want to make water.

00:42:23.107 --> 00:42:24.008
Can I do that?

00:42:24.008 --> 00:42:26.477
And then the carbon
and the nitrogen

00:42:26.477 --> 00:42:27.511
were like, you know what?

00:42:27.511 --> 00:42:29.780
Let me check it out, internally.

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:31.615
Let me check out
my Lewis structure.

00:42:31.615 --> 00:42:34.385
And it's like, yeah, you
can take the OH and the H

00:42:34.385 --> 00:42:38.222
and make water, because then
we could connect and form

00:42:38.222 --> 00:42:39.990
this bond here.

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:43.727
And that bond is
called an amide link.

00:42:48.599 --> 00:42:49.366
That's that kind.

00:42:49.366 --> 00:42:51.635
And so I've formed a kind
of bond between these two

00:42:51.635 --> 00:42:54.271
molecules that's
a covalent bond.

00:42:54.271 --> 00:42:58.275
And this polymer is a class
of polymers called polyamide.

00:43:02.513 --> 00:43:05.049
Because you can
see now, OK, I've

00:43:05.049 --> 00:43:09.219
given off some water,
condensation polarization,

00:43:09.219 --> 00:43:10.754
but I'm also now ready.

00:43:10.754 --> 00:43:13.123
I've got my carboxylic
acid, and my NH,

00:43:13.123 --> 00:43:18.062
my amine groups there
ready to go again.

00:43:18.062 --> 00:43:21.432
So if this side finds
another one of these,

00:43:21.432 --> 00:43:23.701
it can attach and react.

00:43:23.701 --> 00:43:25.936
And if this side finds
another one of those,

00:43:25.936 --> 00:43:26.937
it can attach and react.

00:43:26.937 --> 00:43:31.609
And it keeps going
and going and going.

00:43:31.609 --> 00:43:34.478
In much the same way, it's
just now instead of a radical,

00:43:34.478 --> 00:43:39.149
instead of initiating it
by tearing an electron out,

00:43:39.149 --> 00:43:41.151
I simply have brought
together two types

00:43:41.151 --> 00:43:45.155
of molecules that want
to, in giving up water,

00:43:45.155 --> 00:43:46.590
react and form a bond.

00:43:46.590 --> 00:43:48.459
That's very powerful.

00:43:48.459 --> 00:43:52.062
It's very powerful,
because I can do--

00:43:52.062 --> 00:43:58.469
I can now, like I said, this
can have whatever inside.

00:43:58.469 --> 00:44:01.305
So now it's a different
way of thinking about it.

00:44:01.305 --> 00:44:02.906
Before we had the mer.

00:44:02.906 --> 00:44:04.608
And I could make the
mer whatever I want.

00:44:04.608 --> 00:44:06.276
Just keep a double
bond somewhere,

00:44:06.276 --> 00:44:08.112
and I can make a polymer.

00:44:08.112 --> 00:44:09.713
That's a lot of flexibility.

00:44:09.713 --> 00:44:12.816
Now, instead of
keeping a double bond,

00:44:12.816 --> 00:44:15.786
just keep these amine and
carboxylic acid groups there,

00:44:15.786 --> 00:44:18.122
and I can put anything
inside the boxes that I want.

00:44:18.122 --> 00:44:21.558
Again, massive tunability.

00:44:21.558 --> 00:44:24.895
And one of the funnest things
to do is the nylon rope pull.

00:44:24.895 --> 00:44:25.829
We couldn't do it.

00:44:25.829 --> 00:44:29.833
Apparently, it's a
little dangerous.

00:44:29.833 --> 00:44:36.273
Nylon is when the box winds up
having a carbon chain in it.

00:44:36.273 --> 00:44:40.077
Six carbon atoms.

00:44:40.077 --> 00:44:42.379
And when this has
six carbon atoms

00:44:42.379 --> 00:44:46.850
and this has six
carbon atoms, that's

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:51.989
called nylon 6,6, which
some of you may have seen.

00:44:51.989 --> 00:44:57.695
So you get nylon 6,6, or
sometimes it's just called 66.

00:44:57.695 --> 00:45:04.501
It's six carbon
atoms in the boxes.

00:45:04.501 --> 00:45:06.937
You can imagine you
can make 6,8, 4,6.

00:45:06.937 --> 00:45:07.771
You can play around.

00:45:07.771 --> 00:45:10.207
You can put lots of
things in those boxes.

00:45:10.207 --> 00:45:11.942
The nylon rope pull
is such a cool thing.

00:45:11.942 --> 00:45:13.377
I had to show you a video.

00:45:13.377 --> 00:45:14.578
Here's what you've got.

00:45:14.578 --> 00:45:17.581
You've got one molecule
in solution on the top.

00:45:17.581 --> 00:45:18.115
You can tell.

00:45:18.115 --> 00:45:19.683
It's the slightly
lighter liquid.

00:45:19.683 --> 00:45:21.618
And the other one on the bottom.

00:45:21.618 --> 00:45:25.923
The amines on one side, these
carboxylic acid on the other.

00:45:25.923 --> 00:45:26.724
This is nylon.

00:45:26.724 --> 00:45:29.059
So the box is six carbon atoms.

00:45:29.059 --> 00:45:30.561
Now, what's happening?

00:45:30.561 --> 00:45:37.434
At the interface, this
reaction is occurring.

00:45:37.434 --> 00:45:40.270
So what I can do,
and I'll play this.

00:45:40.270 --> 00:45:45.409
Or what this person can do is
put a little piece of glass

00:45:45.409 --> 00:45:50.481
stir in there and
pull nylon out.

00:45:50.481 --> 00:45:52.916
So watch how he does this.

00:45:52.916 --> 00:45:54.384
So you get it started.

00:45:54.384 --> 00:45:59.123
There is a piece of solid
coming out of two liquids.

00:45:59.123 --> 00:45:59.823
What's happening?

00:45:59.823 --> 00:46:02.359
As he pulls it, more
is reacting and forming

00:46:02.359 --> 00:46:04.795
more of these chains.

00:46:04.795 --> 00:46:09.266
And you can just keep
going and going and going.

00:46:09.266 --> 00:46:10.734
What's so cool
about this is you're

00:46:10.734 --> 00:46:13.237
literally creating this
solid with functionality

00:46:13.237 --> 00:46:15.506
that's tunable out of
these two liquid baths.

00:46:15.506 --> 00:46:17.941
It's all happening
at the interface

00:46:17.941 --> 00:46:21.044
where the two molecules, the
amine and the carboxylic acid

00:46:21.044 --> 00:46:21.812
can see each other.

00:46:21.812 --> 00:46:22.446
And there you can see it.

00:46:22.446 --> 00:46:24.782
I wish you didn't have the
text there, but look at that.

00:46:24.782 --> 00:46:27.084
You're pulling a polymer
right out of the interface

00:46:27.084 --> 00:46:28.685
between two liquids.

00:46:28.685 --> 00:46:30.988
That's how nylon is made.

00:46:30.988 --> 00:46:34.858
And again, this had
an incredible impact

00:46:34.858 --> 00:46:36.493
on so many applications.

00:46:36.493 --> 00:46:37.928
I thought I'd show you this one.

00:46:37.928 --> 00:46:40.964
That's the first
line in New York

00:46:40.964 --> 00:46:43.634
to buy stockings,
nylon stockings.

00:46:43.634 --> 00:46:45.169
And it's going on and on and on.

00:46:45.169 --> 00:46:47.504
Of course, then there are a
few other applications, too,

00:46:47.504 --> 00:46:51.241
like parachutes, and many, many,
many more, because again, it's

00:46:51.241 --> 00:46:53.710
a whole other way
of making polymers.

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:56.213
Now, I'll talk more
about this on Wednesday.

00:46:56.213 --> 00:47:01.819
But in your goody bag,
you've got old school fun.

00:47:01.819 --> 00:47:04.321
And we'll talk about this in
the context of the things we're

00:47:04.321 --> 00:47:06.323
going to learn more about
polymers and polymer

00:47:06.323 --> 00:47:07.758
engineering.

00:47:07.758 --> 00:47:09.560
See you guys on Wednesday.

